translation:
One new song, one cup of wine;
last year's weather, the old pavilion and terrace.
The sun sets in the west — when will it return?

Helplessly the flowers fall away;
seemingly familiar, the swallows come back.
Alone I pace the fragrant path of the small garden.

Fierce wind, high sky, the gibbons' mournful cry;
Clear sandbar, white sand, birds wheel and fly.
Boundless, the falling leaves come rustling down;
Endless, the Yangtze rolls and surges toward me.
Ten thousand miles away, in autumn grief, ever a stranger;
A hundred years of sickness, alone I climb the terrace.
Hardship and bitter grief have thickened frost at my temples;
Worn out, I have of late set down my cup of turbid wine.
